{{Italic title|string=Autopista}}
The '''''autopista''''' ({{IPA|bl|ˈôdōˌpēstə|lang}}; {{literal translation|automobile track}}) are roads forming the [[Balisca|Baliscan]] confederal system of [[W:Motorway|motorways]]. The total length of the system is about {{convert|10812|km}}, as of 1 January 2025. There are also 13 motorway [[W:Spur route|spur routes]], which extend for {{convert|355|km}}.

Most of the Baliscan motorways have two lanes per carriageway, but {{convert|3532|km}} of the Baliscan motorway network have three lanes per carriageway, {{convert|328|km}} have four lanes per carriageway, and only {{convert|5.3|km}} have five lanes per carriageway. The density is of {{convert|7.08|km}} of motorway for every {{convert|1000|km2}} of Balisca territory.

[[Hesperia]] pioneered the construction of the first Baliscan motorways, establishing the so-called ''autostrade'', reserved for fast traffic and for motor vehicles only. The ''[[Autopista del Mar]]'' ('Sea Motorway'), the first built in the archipelago, connecting [[Alessandria]] to [[Cordova]] and [[Guadalajara]], and now forms the [[Autopista 8|A8]] and [[Autopista A9|A9]] motorways, was inaugurated in 1927.

On Baliscan motorways, the [[W:Toll (fee)|toll]] applies to all motorways. There are two types of toll systems used on the ''autopista'': the "closed motorway system" (toll based on kilometres travelled) or the "open motorway system" (flat-rate toll). Since a motorway could be managed by numerous operators, the toll is only requested when exiting the motorway and not when the motorway operator changes.

